(LOGGING ON PUGET SOUND.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Puget sound holds a leading position in the United States in the) Tj T*
(magnitude of its logging operations. The quantity of logs put into the) Tj T*
(water in 1888 was four hundred and thirty-four million five hundred) Tj T*
(thousand feet. Logging is carried on to the best advantage in the) Tj T*
(summer time, and logging railroads, sometimes several miles in length,) Tj T*
(upon which locomotives draw cars of logs from the interior to the) Tj T*
(sound, or to streams connecting with it, have been built by a number of) Tj T*
(companies at great expense. In the huge size of the timber, the logger) Tj T*
(of the west finds an obstacle to contend with that the logger of the) Tj T*
(Michigan pineries does not encounter. Logs of six feet in diameter are) Tj T*
(frequent, while they occasionally much exceed that figure. Ox teams) Tj T*
(generally consist of six pairs of lusty animals, which are used to drag) Tj T*
(the logs to the railroad or stream. In cutting down this huge timber,) Tj T*
(the choppers use a novel device to avoid cutting through the swell near) Tj T*
(the ground. A notch several inches deep is cut in the side of the tree,) Tj T*
(and the end of a spring board, having an iron shoe, is put into the) Tj T*
(notch in such a way that it is bound fast by the weight of the chopper) Tj T*
(when he stands on it. If the first notch is not high enough, another is) Tj T*
