() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Rearing its great mass of snow and ice far above the surrounding) Tj T*
(mountains, Mt. Tacoma is the most commanding object in every Puget) Tj T*
(sound landscape, and is never seen to better advantage than from the) Tj T*
(streets of Tacoma. Its height is fourteen thousand four hundred and) Tj T*
(forty-four feet, exceeding that of any other of the numerous snow) Tj T*
(peaks of the Cascades, and in beauty of form and location it stands) Tj T*
(pre-eminent the monarch of the mountains. Captain George Vancouver,) Tj T*
(the discoverer and original explorer of Puget sound, in May, 1792,) Tj T*
(named this mountain ?Rainier,? in honor of Rear Admiral Rainier, of) Tj T*
(the English navy, but the people of Puget sound, who can see no reason) Tj T*
(why the original and characteristic names given such objects by the) Tj T*
(aborigines should be changed, have discarded that title and restored) Tj T*
(the Indian name ?Tacoma.? It is a beautiful name and most appropriate,) Tj T*
(meaning ?near to heaven.? Ascents of the mountain are very frequently) Tj T*
(made by tourists, arrangements for which can be made in Tacoma. The) Tj T*
(view from its summit is grand beyond description, and the wild and) Tj T*
(rugged nature of its glaciers, gorges, canyons and rocky precipices) Tj T*
(give the mountain climber all the excitement he can reasonably) Tj T*
(desire. Mountain sheep and goats are hunted amid its glaciers by the) Tj T*
(venturesome sportsman, and the forests of the surrounding mountains are) Tj T*
(full of game that will try the nerve and skill of the most experienced) Tj T*
(hunter, no matter from what quarter of the globe he may come.) Tj T*
