(Hops are the leading agricultural product of the Puget sound region,) Tj T*
(and hop ranches are nearly all directly tributary to Tacoma. Puyallup) Tj T*
(valley, whose fame as a hop producing section has encircled the world,) Tj T*
(lies just east of the city, the line of the Northern Pacific passing) Tj T*
(directly through it. Upwards of three thousand acres are now in vine,) Tj T*
(which yielded twenty thousand bales in 1887, and twenty-five thousand) Tj T*
(in 1888, when an average of one thousand seven hundred pounds per acre) Tj T*
(of both old and young vines was secured. The superior quality of the) Tj T*
(cones, the freedom from pests and disease, the enormous yield and the) Tj T*
(rapid growth of young vines, combine to render this region foremost in) Tj T*
(the world in its adaptability to this special crop. Not only does the) Tj T*
(Puyallup valley produce hops, but other crops as well. Several thriving) Tj T*
(villages and numerous highly cultivated farms attest the prosperity of) Tj T*
(its people, as well as giving evidence of the valuable nature of the) Tj T*
(agricultural area immediately tributary to Tacoma. Puyallup hops are) Tj T*
(in demand in Japan, Europe and the eastern states of America, because) Tj T*
(of their superior quality and appearance. In the picking season the) Tj T*
(hop fields are the scene of great industry, and the numerous camps of) Tj T*
(pickers present a pleasing picture as the train passes up the valley.) Tj T*
